  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1244 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nWeak northwest flow aloft and a sfc boundary will allow for isolated \nrain chances this afternoon, especially across southeast Missouri. \nThe boundary lifts north overnight with some improvement to the\nunhealthy air quality conditions across portions of southern \nIllinois and southwest Indiana on Tuesday. However, a strong \ncold front will begin to approach the FA from the northwest with\nincreasing shear from a 40-50 kt jet at 700-500 mb. While most \nof the CAMs show the bulk of scattered convection occurring \nduring the afternoon hours, the FV3/ARW have a decaying complex\nof storms moving across southwest Indiana after 12z. Meanwhile,\nthe HRRR/NSSL show scattered development mainly south of the \nOhio confined to the afternoon hours. Despite the uncertainty, \nthe risk for isolated strong to severe storms will be the \ngreatest between 12-7PM when 4000 to 5000 J/kg of MLCAPE is \nprogged by the RAP13 along with 7.0 C/km sfc-3km lapse rate. \nAlthough the best shear will be lagging behind the front, a \nrobust theta-e difference of 30-40K will favor 60-70 mph \ndamaging wind gusts as the main hazard of concern. Torrential \ndownpours will also be capable of causing localized flash \nflooding issues with runoff on roadways.\n\nA Heat Advisory and Extreme Heat Warning are now in effect through \nTuesday as heat index values between 110 to 116 are progged for \ntomorrow. However, locations along I-64 are only under a Heat \nAdvisory as the earlier timing of storms will help to keep heat \nindices a bit cooler in the 105 to 109 range. High temperatures\nin the low to mid 90s are expected over most of the FA setting \nthe stage for the hottest day of the week. The air quality will \nalso still be unhealthy across portions of southern Illinois and\nsouthwest Indiana due to the wildfires in Canada. Temperatures \nwill then be 10 degrees cooler behind the front on Wednesday \nwith highs only in the low to mid 80s. Humidity levels will \nalso be significantly lower with dewpoints dipping down into the\n60s and 50s. Rain chances return on Friday as a shortwave moves\nthrough, followed by a significant warming trend with an \nincrease in humidity over the weekend as the eastern extent of a\n597 dm ridge over the southern Plains builds in. Temperatures by\nearly next week will once again reach the 90s.",
